# Managed by Ansible (roles/hardening). AIDE rules for logs lamware adds to the host.
#
# Without these, the logs below fall to `/ 0 Full` (99_aide_root): every append
# is a change and every rotation is a set of adds, removes and changes, so the
# nightly check would alert every night. These follow the pattern of the
# package rules in 31_aide_*: the active log may grow (but not shrink or change
# owner), and each rotated copy must keep its content as it moves down the chain.
# Measured on the sandbox 2026-10-25 against a check 7 minutes after a rebaseline.

# Process accounting (acct): /etc/cron.daily/acct runs `savelog -c 30` at 06:25:
# pacct -> pacct.0 -> pacct.1.gz -> ... -> pacct.29.gz (dropped next day).
/var/log/account$ d VarDir
/var/log/account/pacct$ f ActLog
/var/log/account/pacct\.0$ f RotLog
/var/log/account/pacct\.1\.@@{LOGEXT}$ f CompSerLog
/var/log/account/pacct\.([2-9]|1[0-9]|2[0-8])\.@@{LOGEXT}$ f MidlSerLog
/var/log/account/pacct\.29\.@@{LOGEXT}$ f LastSerLog

# sudo's log (Defaults logfile, sudoers.d/012_logfile): appended, not rotated.
/var/log/sudo\.log$ f ActLog

# PostgreSQL: weekly copytruncate (so the active file shrinks in place), dateext,
# delaycompress with xz: main.log-YYYYMMDD for a week, then main.log-YYYYMMDD.xz.
/var/log/postgresql$ d VarDir
/var/log/postgresql/postgresql-[0-9]+-main\.log$ f FreqRotLog
/var/log/postgresql/postgresql-[0-9]+-main\.log-[0-9]{8}(\.xz)?$ f LowDELog

# Not a log, but the same kind of rule: nuclei (security-test) creates and removes
# entries in /root on every run, which changes the directory's mtime. VarDir
# drops the times and keeps owner, mode and inode; everything inside /root stays
# on Full (an added or changed file there is still reported).
/root$ d VarDir
